# The three-valued X = Y of balls with one-limb centers (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface",
# "Comparisons"): yes when every member of X equals every member of Y, that is both balls the same
# point, r_x = r_y = 0 and c_x = c_y; no when no member of X equals one of Y, the balls disjoint,
# r_x + r_y < |c_y - c_x|, by lt_2_2 on the exact pairs. Both false for an empty operand and for the
# whole line. Exact when r_x + r_y <= Omega.
kernel:        ball_equal_1
input:         stored cx
input:         stored rx
input:         stored cy
input:         stored ry
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      ball cx rx
requires:      ball cy ry
ensures:       trit yes no eq x y x=cx,rx y=cy,ry exact_when=abs_le(rx+ry,omega)
min_precision: 2
source:        the radii and the centers compared, and the sum of the radii with the distance of the centers as exact pairs (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeBallPredicates.v ball_equal_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the results are masks, which have no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical ball

ea       = eq(rx, 0)
eb       = eq(ry, 0)
ec       = eq(cx, cy)
eab      = and(ea, eb)
yes      = and(eab, ec)
(s0, s1) = two_sum_ord(rx, ry)
ncx      = neg(cx)
(d0, d1) = two_sum_ord(cy, ncx)
(a0, a1) = abs_2(d0, d1)
no       = lt_2_2(s0, s1, a0, a1)
